// Demonstrates the datetime type: a naive timestamp
// (date + time-of-day, no timezone), comparable exactly like date but
// finer-grained. clock_in/clock_out are written space-separated, the
// same shape a timeclock or bank export typically uses, so formats
// names the layout explicitly for both -- reusing date's own kwarg, not
// a second one. filter(.clock_out < .clock_in) flags a shift whose
// clock-out is chronologically before its clock-in: almost always a
// data-entry error (forgetting to roll the date to the next day for an
// overnight shift), not something date alone could catch once the two
// times share a calendar date.
source in = csv("datetime.csv",
  schema:  { name: string, clock_in: datetime, clock_out: datetime },
  formats: { clock_in: "2006-01-02 15:04:05", clock_out: "2006-01-02 15:04:05" }
)
sink out = jsonl("datetime_out.jsonl")

pipeline main {
  in |> filter(.clock_out < .clock_in) |> out
}
